            <ArticleTitle>Pain on injection of propofol: the effect of granisetron pretreatment.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>121-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess the effect of granisetron pretreatment in alleviating propofol injection pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">A randomized, controlled, double-blind study, using venous retention with a tourniquet.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">One hundred fifty adult patients were randomly assigned to one of three groups: group 1 (who received 5 mL of 0.9% saline pretreatment), group 2 (who received 5 mL lidocaine [40 mg in 0.9% saline] pretreatment), and group 3 (who received 5 mL granisetron [2 mg in 0.9% saline] pretreatment). Injections were given in the largest vein on the dorsum of the hand. After 2 minutes, the tourniquet was released and one fourth of the total calculated dose of propofol (2.5 mg/kg body weight) was administered and pain assessment was made.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Lidocaine and granisetron significantly reduced the incidence and severity of propofol injection pain more than placebo (P &lt; 0.001). The efficacy of granisetron in alleviating the pain on injection of propofol was no different from lidocaine.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Granisetron pretreatment may be used to reduce the incidence of pain on injection of propofol, an advantage added to the useful prevention of postoperative nausea and vomiting.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
